{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And houses full of all good things, which you did not fill, etc., vineyards and olive trees, which you did not plant.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: even pig walls, even vineyards planted as orlah — all of it He permitted to them. And so it was the law of the Torah that all forbidden foods were permitted in the booty of those who went forth to war, for the seven years that they conquered, apart from idolatry, which He did not permit to them." "<b>And the Rambam</b>, of blessed memory, wrote in the Laws of Kings and Their Wars: when combat troops enter the territory of the nations, it is permitted to them to eat neveilot and treifot or the flesh of swine, if he is hungry and finds nothing to eat except forbidden foods, and likewise he may drink yayin nesekh. End quote." "<b>Now</b> this is surprising concerning the Rav, of blessed memory, for the Torah certainly permitted all forbidden foods as though permitted, even if he has something else to eat. So what does the Rav, of blessed memory, mean by 'if he is hungry'? Did the Torah permit them only on account of danger to life? Even without danger to life the Torah permitted them. The Ramban, of blessed memory, already took issue with him on this, and likewise concerning what he said, that he may drink yayin nesekh — and he said that it is not true that it should be permitted to drink yayin nesekh, for yayin nesekh is among the prohibitions of idolatry. And if the Rav intended ordinary wine of theirs, he need not have stated it, for if Torah prohibitions were permitted, all the more so their ordinary wine, which is of Rabbinic origin."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:46:05.266745205Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}